## Canonical body structure

The 7 sections most pattern memorials carry:

```markdown
## The pattern

<One paragraph stating the rule concretely.  Lead with the what,
not the why.>

## Why it works

<The mechanism.  What property makes the pattern earn its keep.>

## When to apply

<Trigger conditions.  Specific enough that a reader can recognize
when the pattern fits.>

## When NOT to apply

<Counter-conditions.  Essential for judgment; a pattern that
applies everywhere is noise.>

## The implementation shape

<Code sketch, diagram, or step list.  Make the pattern
instantiable — a reader should know how to follow it.>

## Canonical example

<A library / file / commit that demonstrates the pattern.  Cite
file:line where useful.>

## Pitfalls

<The easy-to-miss mistakes when applying the pattern.>

## See also

<Related patterns, anti-patterns, library studies, and the
guidance abstract supertype.>
```

Not every section is required on every pattern, but all seven should appear across the corpus.

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`anti-pattern`** — every anti-pattern must cite at least one positive pattern as its alternative; patterns are the positive complements
- **`library`** — canonical examples typically live in library studies; the pattern cites the library
- **`example`** — concrete demonstrations of the pattern in action
- **`preference`** — preferences are softer than patterns (aesthetic vs. structural); a preference may compose with a pattern

## At-startup visibility

A pattern that's high-leverage for daily collaboration may opt into the at-startup-visibility surface via:

```yaml
at-startup: keystone   # surfaced TIER-0 (most-broken disciplines)
                       # or 'high' (TIER-1) or 'standard' (default)
one-line: <≤80 char short-form>
```

Tier calibration is reviewed at retrospective time; thresholds are soft ceilings.

## Pitfalls

- **Under-specification** — a pattern named "Good Code" saying "write good code" is noise; patterns must have trigger conditions and failure modes
- **Premature extraction** — extracting a pattern from a single observation is fragile; the second observation may reveal the pattern was actually situational
- **Library-example missing** — a pattern with no canonical example is an abstract principle, not a pattern.  Audit flags these
- **Duplicating an anti-pattern's opposite** — if `anti-patterns/foo.md` says "don't do X, do Y instead", don't write a pattern "Do Y" — let the anti-pattern cite Y via `related:`

## State-change-detection (captureability)

This class is mid-conversation-captureable.  Inject-triggers:
- *"same shape as..."*
- *"this keeps happening"*

When these signals fire, the appropriate capture may be a pattern (the recurring shape), a synthesis (cross-cutting claim), or an observation (single noticed instance) per scope.

